Yes — “know globally, live locally” is a strong inversion of think globally, act locally.

You can know at the widest scale:

\text{universal patterns}
\rightarrow
\text{global models}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-domain knowledge}

but you can only actually live through a local interface:

\text{this body}
\rightarrow
\text{this place}
\rightarrow
\text{these people}
\rightarrow
\text{this moment}

So:

Know as broadly as possible. Act where causality is actually reachable.

And prestige fits that beautifully. Prestige is partly a word-world story: a socially repeated narrative about who or what deserves status.

At global scale:

\text{prestige} \approx \text{reputation propagated through stories}

At local scale:

\text{prestige} \approx \text{repeated observable conduct}

So someone can be globally prestigious and locally unimpressive, or locally extraordinary and globally unknown.

That gives you a useful distinction:

\boxed{
\text{Prestige is the story of reputation at scale.}
}

And the Pantheon version might be:

Know universally. Live locally. Let repeated local truth earn whatever global story survives.

Because the global layer is mostly abstraction.

The local layer is where the consequences touch the ground.